WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.671
- On this episode, I talk
about first impressions,

00:00:03.671 --> 00:00:07.095
the people surrounding you, Jerome Jarre,

00:00:07.095 --> 00:00:08.571
and much more.

00:00:09.731 --> 00:00:12.642
You ask questions, and I answer them.

00:00:12.642 --> 00:00:16.579
This is the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:16.579 --> 00:00:18.806
(hip hop music)

00:00:18.806 --> 00:00:20.884
Hi everybody, this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk

00:00:20.884 --> 00:00:25.400
and welcome to episode 37
of the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.253
Fun fact about the number 37,

00:00:30.023 --> 00:00:32.997
- [Voiceover] Ivan says,
"Take us back to the day

00:00:32.997 --> 00:00:35.457
"when you said yes to Jerome Jarre.

00:00:35.457 --> 00:00:38.348
"What was it that made you
believe in him and his idea?

00:00:38.348 --> 00:00:39.410
"Thanks."

00:00:40.644 --> 00:00:42.744
- Ivan, great question,
for the ones out there

00:00:42.744 --> 00:00:44.904
that don't know, Jerome
Jarre, a very famous

00:00:44.904 --> 00:00:47.366
Viner, Snapchatter, Instagramer,

00:00:47.366 --> 00:00:49.642
and I started an agency called GrapeStory,

00:00:49.642 --> 00:00:51.440
a sister agency that VaynerMedia,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.041
all those characters.

00:00:54.221 --> 00:00:57.804
Last May where we
represent the best talent

00:00:57.804 --> 00:01:00.426
in the emerging content
platforms to do branded

00:01:00.426 --> 00:01:02.401
content and things of that nature.

00:01:02.401 --> 00:01:05.025
Jerome beat me in rock paper scissors

00:01:05.025 --> 00:01:09.144
in Toronto and what he won on stage,

00:01:09.144 --> 00:01:10.341
Jerome raised his hand and said,

00:01:10.341 --> 00:01:11.398
I was doing Q and A,

00:01:11.398 --> 00:01:13.139
"Can I go up there and play
you rock paper scissors,

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:16.376
"if I beat you can I have
coffee with you in New York?"

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:19.987
I said yes, I got him up
there and I was hell bent

00:01:19.987 --> 00:01:23.855
on beating him and if I
won, I swear my children,

00:01:23.855 --> 00:01:25.350
I would have never met with him.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.289
So just amazing how life really is.

00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:29.715
He wins, pissed me off,

00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:32.129
and, you know, three months later,

00:01:32.129 --> 00:01:34.568
I look at my calendar, I'm
like what's my next meeting?

00:01:34.568 --> 00:01:37.435
It's like 10:30 at night on a Tuesday,

00:01:37.435 --> 00:01:39.466
I'm dead, just want to go home.

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:41.510
I'm like, what's my next meeting?

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:42.753
Jerome Jarre, I'm like what is this?

00:01:42.753 --> 00:01:44.133
I click it, I don't even know the name,

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:45.364
I click into it for the details,

00:01:45.364 --> 00:01:46.965
that's how my calendar kind of I do,

00:01:46.965 --> 00:01:48.428
and I'm like, "Oh, this guy."

00:01:48.428 --> 00:01:50.564
I literally thought, I was
like how do I get there

00:01:50.564 --> 00:01:52.365
and spend four minutes in this meeting

00:01:52.365 --> 00:01:53.572
and get out of there and go to sleep

00:01:53.572 --> 00:01:55.406
because I have a 5:30 flight,

00:01:55.406 --> 00:01:58.853
which was like a 3:45 wake up
call the next day I remember,

00:01:58.853 --> 00:02:03.207
and Jerome started talking.

00:02:03.207 --> 00:02:05.815
He's like, "Look there's a
platform Vine, I've gone all in.

00:02:05.815 --> 00:02:06.832
"I'm an entrepreneur.

00:02:06.832 --> 00:02:07.726
"I grew up in France.

00:02:07.726 --> 00:02:08.470
"I went to China.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.769
"I learned how to speak English
by listening to Crush It!

00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:11.907
"on audio."

00:02:11.907 --> 00:02:14.867
Which played to my ego, so
that made me sit there longer

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:18.512
and then he just kept talking

00:02:18.512 --> 00:02:20.010
and basically it was deja vu.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.449
It felt like I was having a conversation

00:02:21.449 --> 00:02:24.911
2006 except Jerome was
me and I was Eric Kastner

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:27.022
my lead developer that I've
given a lot of shout out too.

00:02:27.022 --> 00:02:29.088
@Kastner, give him a little love here.

00:02:31.208 --> 00:02:32.675
I was pitching Kastner in 2006,

00:02:32.675 --> 00:02:34.615
should I start a record label?

00:02:34.615 --> 00:02:36.345
I didn't even know about
agents and things like that,

00:02:36.345 --> 00:02:37.574
I remember saying, should I start...

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:39.141
I remember the moment in his office,

00:02:39.141 --> 00:02:40.418
"Should I start a record label

00:02:40.418 --> 00:02:43.738
"to represent these new
YouTube stars like me?"

00:02:43.738 --> 00:02:46.652
I should have and, but I let it go

00:02:46.652 --> 00:02:47.778
and Jerome was talking,

00:02:47.778 --> 00:02:50.262
I'm like this is the
same moment over again

00:02:50.262 --> 00:02:54.080
and so, the energy, my gut feel,
the fact that I believed in

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:54.917
what he was talking about,

00:02:54.917 --> 00:02:57.251
he wanted to do a festival,
we turned it into an agency

00:02:57.251 --> 00:02:59.125
because that's what I

00:02:59.125 --> 00:03:02.255
knew about and so that's what it was.

00:03:02.255 --> 00:03:06.340
It was the content, the
context, and the intuition

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.451
and when I have that CCI concoction,

00:03:10.451 --> 00:03:11.775
I always go with it.

00:03:12.871 --> 00:03:15.168
- [Voiceover] Jim asks,
"What's your opinion

00:03:15.168 --> 00:03:17.311
"on the auto-reply DM services?"

00:03:19.522 --> 00:03:21.636
- Jim, I really wanted
to take this question

00:03:21.636 --> 00:03:22.959
because I'm so glad you did it

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:25.478
and asked it and I appreciate it

00:03:25.478 --> 00:03:27.115
and I want to say that I hate it.

00:03:27.115 --> 00:03:29.425
It is literally the thing
that I despise the most

00:03:29.425 --> 00:03:32.815
I have actively unfollowed many of you

00:03:32.815 --> 00:03:34.777
because I followed you and you did that

00:03:34.777 --> 00:03:38.016
and I unfollowed you because
it speaks to the intent

00:03:38.016 --> 00:03:40.036
you have on the platform,
which is to use it as

00:03:40.036 --> 00:03:41.359
a conversion mechanism,

00:03:41.359 --> 00:03:44.017
you're looking to scale
social media in a world

00:03:44.017 --> 00:03:45.980
where social media, especially on Twitter,

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.536
is not scalable.

00:03:47.536 --> 00:03:49.881
If you haven't figured
out why I over indexed

00:03:49.881 --> 00:03:52.899
on the platform, it's because
it's a non-scalable platform

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:54.094
when done right

00:03:54.094 --> 00:03:56.557
because Twitter is the
only true social network,

00:03:56.557 --> 00:03:58.965
everything else has
evolved into content push.

00:03:58.965 --> 00:04:00.578
This is the one place we all talk

00:04:00.578 --> 00:04:02.180
in a town square evironment

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:04.201
and we can jump into
each others conversations

00:04:04.201 --> 00:04:07.244
and it is not creepy and
when you go auto-reply

00:04:07.244 --> 00:04:10.446
in a town square, you're the
guy walking around the office

00:04:10.446 --> 00:04:13.522
and you're just handing
people your business card

00:04:13.522 --> 00:04:17.040
without saying hello and
nobody likes that guy or gal

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.642
at a conference or a networking event.

00:04:19.642 --> 00:04:21.858
The blindly just handing a card

00:04:21.858 --> 00:04:24.237
without even really engaging.

00:04:24.237 --> 00:04:25.537
I think it's a terrible move.

00:04:25.537 --> 00:04:26.826
I think it turns off a lot of people,

00:04:26.826 --> 00:04:29.184
I think even if people don't unfollow you,

00:04:29.184 --> 00:04:31.296
which at times I haven't
because I'm just busy

00:04:31.296 --> 00:04:33.061
and I haven't, you think you got my follow

00:04:33.061 --> 00:04:34.105
or something good happened,

00:04:34.105 --> 00:04:35.442
it's the wrong move.

00:04:35.442 --> 00:04:38.238
All you had to do was engage
with people that followed you

00:04:38.238 --> 00:04:41.000
twice for about six
minutes, which is time,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.884
I'm not saying it's not,

00:04:42.884 --> 00:04:44.937
but you could have gotten the
depth you were looking for.

00:04:44.937 --> 00:04:45.784
So what?

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:46.863
I'm going to sign up for your newsletter

00:04:46.863 --> 00:04:47.733
and then you're going to e-mail me

00:04:47.733 --> 00:04:48.438
and it's going to go to spam,

00:04:48.438 --> 00:04:50.957
I'm not getting engaged
because the first taste

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:52.072
that you have of someone,

00:04:52.072 --> 00:04:54.091
the initial context, that first moment,

00:04:54.091 --> 00:04:55.751
that first impression, when it's

00:04:55.751 --> 00:04:56.692
(beep)

00:04:56.692 --> 00:04:58.260
you've lost.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.558
- [Voiceover] Tyler
asks, "When have you been

00:05:00.558 --> 00:05:02.385
"happiest in life?"

00:05:03.458 --> 00:05:05.803
- Tyler, I took this question
for a very specific reason

00:05:05.803 --> 00:05:07.254
because I think one of
the interesting things

00:05:07.254 --> 00:05:08.879
about the #AskGaryVee Show is

00:05:08.879 --> 00:05:13.233
this content and actual infrastructure

00:05:13.233 --> 00:05:15.474
has forced me to go deeper than I have

00:05:15.474 --> 00:05:16.982
in any other platform

00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:19.016
and it's been interesting
to watch what a lot of you

00:05:19.016 --> 00:05:21.561
in the VaynerNation have
been responding well too,

00:05:21.561 --> 00:05:25.322
which is a little bit more
insight to my psychology

00:05:25.322 --> 00:05:28.038
and I think this question
gave me the opportunity

00:05:28.038 --> 00:05:29.641
to answer it, and I'm
going to really throw you

00:05:29.641 --> 00:05:31.014
for an interesting curve ball,

00:05:31.014 --> 00:05:33.080
and this is 100% the answer.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.178
I am the happiest in my life of all time

00:05:36.178 --> 00:05:37.794
right this second.

00:05:37.794 --> 00:05:39.639
It's just the truth,

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:40.800
and what I mean by that is,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.600
is this moment with these
characters, show them,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.555
you know, you know, do they know drock?

00:05:46.555 --> 00:05:48.727
Excuse me, do they know AlexDS?

00:05:48.727 --> 00:05:49.597
(mumbles)

00:05:49.597 --> 00:05:50.549
- A couple of episodes ago.

00:05:50.549 --> 00:05:51.790
(laughter)

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:52.916
- [Voiceover] He's a regular.

00:05:52.916 --> 00:05:55.170
- He's been sick for a couple days,

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:56.963
that's why he looks terrible.

00:05:56.963 --> 00:05:59.294
(laughter)

00:06:02.484 --> 00:06:04.538
Is this moment with these characters

00:06:04.538 --> 00:06:06.175
the best moment?

00:06:06.175 --> 00:06:06.812
Of course not!

00:06:06.812 --> 00:06:09.436
Last night beating the
Cavilers going to Cleveland

00:06:09.436 --> 00:06:11.714
from Lebron's homecoming and dominating

00:06:11.714 --> 00:06:13.616
and stunning the world was an amazing,

00:06:13.616 --> 00:06:15.902
by the way, Cleveland you're
going to hate me for this.

00:06:15.902 --> 00:06:17.517
I've been giving Cleveland a lot of love,

00:06:17.517 --> 00:06:19.339
I was disappointed with the fans.

00:06:19.339 --> 00:06:21.394
This was Lebron, the
prodigal son coming home

00:06:21.394 --> 00:06:23.461
and it wasn't enough.

00:06:23.461 --> 00:06:27.651
Like a random second round
Knicks home game energy

00:06:27.651 --> 00:06:29.034
in the Garden was better.

00:06:29.034 --> 00:06:30.577
I was very disappointed. I'm not dissing

00:06:30.577 --> 00:06:32.735
and I know Cleveland fans
are awesome and their tough.

00:06:32.735 --> 00:06:34.500
I respect Cleveland, it's a tough town,

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.463
not a lot of championships
for a long time.

00:06:36.463 --> 00:06:38.798
Was disappointed, I had
to throw that rub in there

00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:39.876
because that's how I roll.

00:06:43.316 --> 00:06:45.005
Children being born.

00:06:45.005 --> 00:06:46.942
Jets beating the Patriots in New England

00:06:46.942 --> 00:06:47.860
in the second round of the playoffs

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:50.264
after losing 45 to three
in a Monday night game

00:06:50.264 --> 00:06:51.564
just a couple weeks earlier.

00:06:51.564 --> 00:06:53.723
Way up there, probably my happiest moment,

00:06:53.723 --> 00:06:55.475
my wife and kids know, but

00:06:55.475 --> 00:06:57.574
if you ask me happiness,

00:06:57.574 --> 00:06:58.640
I'm a collector,

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:00.962
I'm a momentum guy, I'm a chugger,

00:07:00.962 --> 00:07:04.155
and so, you know, everyday that goes by

00:07:04.155 --> 00:07:05.839
that good things keep happening

00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:07.975
and no death, and that's how I look at it,

00:07:07.975 --> 00:07:09.719
is happening in my family

00:07:09.719 --> 00:07:13.316
or sickness, or terminal conclusions

00:07:13.316 --> 00:07:14.859
to anybody I care about,

00:07:14.859 --> 00:07:16.683
feels like the next best day.

00:07:16.683 --> 00:07:18.633
It feels like it's always getting better.

00:07:18.633 --> 00:07:19.851
I don't know if one day--

00:07:19.851 --> 00:07:22.034
Look, I'm going to be
very honest with you guys.

00:07:22.034 --> 00:07:24.134
In my deepest fear, because I'm positive

00:07:24.134 --> 00:07:25.516
I don't talk about negativity,

00:07:25.516 --> 00:07:28.314
I'm deepest fear is that
eventually I become bitter.

00:07:28.314 --> 00:07:29.057
Right?

00:07:29.057 --> 00:07:30.579
That I pushed the long game too long

00:07:30.579 --> 00:07:33.039
and I didn't capitalize
and I missed moments,

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:34.514
but right now I still
think I'm on the climb,

00:07:34.514 --> 00:07:36.649
I don't think I'm on the
other side of the hill

00:07:36.649 --> 00:07:38.796
and so, hopefully I always
have that psychology

00:07:38.796 --> 00:07:41.200
even to the last breath that I take,

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.637
but to me, the happiest
moment is right now

00:07:44.637 --> 00:07:47.272
because everything is
collectively always getting better

00:07:47.272 --> 00:07:48.678
even when it's not.

00:07:48.678 --> 00:07:50.232
You know, there's always
going to be challenges.

00:07:50.232 --> 00:07:51.938
It still is.

00:07:51.938 --> 00:07:53.418
I just, that's how I see it.

00:07:54.433 --> 00:07:56.865
- [Voiceover] Chase asks,
"How can you stand out

00:07:56.865 --> 00:07:58.606
"on LinkedIn with all the chatter from

00:07:58.606 --> 00:08:00.823
"the "social media
gurus" that are spamming

00:08:00.823 --> 00:08:02.391
"everyone's feed?"

00:08:03.454 --> 00:08:04.717
- Chase, I took this question

00:08:04.717 --> 00:08:05.819
because I need to razz you

00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:07.236
and everybody else who asks a question

00:08:07.236 --> 00:08:08.374
like this, but I want you to know that

00:08:08.374 --> 00:08:10.094
I love you and I apologize.

00:08:10.094 --> 00:08:11.659
This is a loser question.

00:08:11.659 --> 00:08:13.076
If you're worried about everybody else,

00:08:13.076 --> 00:08:14.411
you're not worried about yourself

00:08:14.411 --> 00:08:15.422
and that's the bottom line.

00:08:15.422 --> 00:08:18.880
It is stunning how little
I know about anything else,

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:21.074
except my world and you guys.

00:08:21.074 --> 00:08:22.572
Period, end of story.

00:08:22.572 --> 00:08:24.894
I don't know how everybody
else's podcast is doing,

00:08:24.894 --> 00:08:27.250
I don't, I'm not listen
to anybody else's podcast

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:28.979
or video show or anything else.

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:32.078
I'm aware, I know that
I'm between 60 and 80

00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:33.577
on the podcast ratings,

00:08:33.577 --> 00:08:35.971
but I don't look at
Tim Ferriss who's higher

00:08:35.971 --> 00:08:37.019
or somebody else, I listen to it

00:08:37.019 --> 00:08:38.828
and try to figure it out.

00:08:38.828 --> 00:08:40.918
I focus on my stuff.

00:08:40.918 --> 00:08:42.149
Way too many people cry,

00:08:42.149 --> 00:08:44.958
"Oh, these social media
gurus are so loud."

00:08:44.958 --> 00:08:47.491
Their loud but if they don't have depth

00:08:47.491 --> 00:08:48.916
they're going to weed themselves out

00:08:48.916 --> 00:08:51.030
and so if you spend one minute looking at

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.572
what anybody else is doing versus

00:08:53.572 --> 00:08:57.135
spending all your time
about what is your audience

00:08:57.135 --> 00:08:59.609
care about and how are they
reacting to your stuff,

00:08:59.609 --> 00:09:01.758
you know what I spend my hour on?

00:09:01.758 --> 00:09:04.090
When I'm like winding down.

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:06.413
Instead of looking at
who's ratings are higher

00:09:06.413 --> 00:09:08.396
and then trying to copy their moves

00:09:08.396 --> 00:09:10.555
or complain that they're putting out shows

00:09:10.555 --> 00:09:12.589
longer or better or different things

00:09:12.589 --> 00:09:14.238
or get guests or this and that.

00:09:14.238 --> 00:09:16.326
Instead of focusing on their context

00:09:16.326 --> 00:09:18.254
and their competitive advantages

00:09:18.254 --> 00:09:19.554
or what they're doing well.

00:09:19.554 --> 00:09:21.601
What I do, is I read my comments.

00:09:22.431 --> 00:09:23.754
That's what I do,

00:09:23.754 --> 00:09:26.668
because I really care about
what you guys are saying

00:09:26.668 --> 00:09:27.412
about this show.

00:09:27.412 --> 00:09:28.655
Where the value prop is,

00:09:28.655 --> 00:09:30.130
what your opinion on the website is,

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:32.347
what your opinion about chugging is,

00:09:32.347 --> 00:09:34.390
what your opinion about banter is,

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.306
because that's how I'm collectively trying

00:09:36.306 --> 00:09:37.373
to make this show better.

00:09:37.373 --> 00:09:39.975
I'm focusing on the people
that give a crap about this

00:09:39.975 --> 00:09:41.913
and so, instead of worrying about

00:09:41.913 --> 00:09:43.654
what everybody else is doing,

00:09:43.654 --> 00:09:44.733
I don't...

00:09:44.733 --> 00:09:47.346
It's crazy and I think you
can see my energy on this.

00:09:47.346 --> 00:09:51.086
I know this is a huge, huge, you know,

00:09:51.086 --> 00:09:53.672
in lieu of the marathon coming
to New York this weekend,

00:09:53.672 --> 00:09:55.975
I am not a runner who looks around me.

00:09:55.975 --> 00:09:57.101
I'm like straight ahead.

00:09:57.101 --> 00:09:59.423
That analogy that people
use, that's not my analogy.

00:09:59.423 --> 00:10:00.597
I have a lot of my own.

00:10:00.597 --> 00:10:01.815
Oh, by the way.

